  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 225 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nHigh-Res guidance shows rain lingering over areas along/west of\nHighway 65 on Thursday morning/afternoon as the upper low opens\nup into a wave and pushes further east towards the area. By \nThursday evening, however, an associated surface cold front will\nbegin to push southeast through the area, pushing the \nshowers/thunderstorms further east through the rest of the \nforecast area Thursday night into Friday morning/early afternoon.\nMean PWATs between 1.5-1.8\" (higher west of Hwy 65) will lead \nto pretty sufficient rainfall. For reference, sounding \nclimatology depicts this to be pretty high for this time of year\n(and a record if it comes to fruition), as the 90th percentile \nis 1.40\", with a daily max of 1.49\". Despite this, with the \ncurrent D2 (Severe) and D3 (Extreme) drought coupled with \nguidance pulling back on rainfall amounts, decided not to move \nforward with any Flood headlines with this forecast package. \nCurrent NBM 48-hour QPF probabilities are the following:\n\n>1.0\": 65-95% west of Highway 65; 35-65% east of Hwy 65\n>2.0\": 40-80% west of Highway 65; 15-25% east of Hwy 65\n>3.0\": 15-45% west of Highway 65; <10% east of Hwy 65\n\nSummarizing that up, the majority of the rain is expected to\nfall west of Highway 65, especially further west of the I-49\ncorridor. HREF LPMM shows a swath of heavy rainfall between\n1.5-2.5\" with localized pockets up to 5+\" extending from\nWichita/southeast of Wichita northeast through Kansas\nCity/northeast MO. If this were to occur, then the heaviest axis\nwould fall just outside our forecast area. This further impacted\nthe decision to hold off on any flood products with this\npackage. We'll continue to monitor the need for a Flood Watch.\nIf guidance suggest the heavy rainfall axis falling further into\nour CWA, then a Watch may be warranted for areas generally\nalong/west of I-49. Regardless, we'll be seeing some much needed\nrainfall, which will hopefully help alleviate at least some of \nthe drought conditions.\n\nTemperatures on Thursday will be vastly different across the\nCWA, with a 10-15 degree spread from west to east. With \nrainfall lingering over areas west of Highway 65 much of the \nday, temperatures are expected to remain in the 70s. East of \nHighway 65 will climb into the low to upper 80s (increasing \ncloser to south-central MO), as rainfall will hold off until \nlater in the evening. \n\nA cooler airmass will push into the region behind the cold\nfront, bringing more widespread seasonable temperatures in the \n70s through the weekend and into the beginning of next week.",
